{"caseId":"OD2-HO-STOP-2","answers":[{"itemId":"I-H1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H2","answer":"contradicted","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H3","answer":"supported","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-H4","answer":"unknown","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E6","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-R1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":82,"evidenceIds":["E2"]},{"itemId":"I-R2","answer":"causal","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["E1"]},{"itemId":"I-R3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E3","E5"]},{"itemId":"I-R4","answer":"association-only","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E5"]},{"itemId":"I-A1","answer":"preferred","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-A2","answer":"harmful","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A3","answer":"harmful","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["E2","E1"]},{"itemId":"I-A4","answer":"premature","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E6"]},{"itemId":"I-A5","answer":"acceptable","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-SUFF","answer":"sufficient","confidence":68,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3","E5"]}],"brief":"Analyzer B most likely had one chance QC outlier (or a one-off sampling glitch), not a real fault. The repeat passed straight away. QC ran on schedule with no other rule violations. Level 2 QC averages are unchanged since the lot change. Analyzer A, on the old lot, agreed with B on 43 retested samples within 0.02 mmol/L on average. B's patient median is stable. So the lot change, skipped QC and a systematic fault are all unsupported. A one-in-300 flag is expected about once or twice a month.

Ward 6's high potassiums are split evenly between A and B. Most of them had long transport times since the tube station failed on 10 Nov. Renal overflow patients are another possible factor. The tube delay is a plausible cause but not proven, because no repeat samples were taken.

I'd sign off: return B to service, close the 186-result review with no amendments, and don't recall, quarantine the lot or widen limits. Don't label the cause as a confirmed sampling disturbance. Optionally add extra Level 2 QC for a week. Ask Ward 6 to recollect samples, and get the tube fixed. I'm moderately confident, around 70%."}
